# Line terminators are DECLARED here, never inherited from whichever machine
# or git install happens to be running. Operator's ruling, 2026-09-03: this is
# a Windows estate and the record is CRLF.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `core.autocrlf` was unset in every scope, so the
# answer to "what terminator does this file have" depended on the client
# reading it. The same SEAT_LOG.md read as clean on the operator's terminal
# and as 3,000 changed lines from a Linux sandbox -- one file, two truths,
# and neither of them written down anywhere. An attribute in the repo travels
# with the repo; a git config does not.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES. Git stores every text blob LF (the portable form, and what
# HEAD already holds) and checks it out CRLF. The working tree is CRLF, the
# history is clean, and a terminator flip stops showing up as a whole-file
# diff. Nothing here changes what the CHAIN writes -- that is declared in the
# code, newline="\r\n" at every writer, and stroked by
# test_the_chain_writes_declared_newlines.

* text=auto eol=crlf


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# SEALED BY HASH -- no conversion, in either direction.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXACTLY the files whose RAW BYTES are hashed, and nothing else.
#
# law.py:63 `_fingerprint()` opens a law document "rb" and sha256s it; the
# ledger binds that digest link by link. An eol translation here changes
# every fingerprint and `python law/law.py verify` goes red on a chain
# nobody tampered with -- a false alarm on the one instrument whose whole
# value is that it does not raise them. Re-terminating a law is a RE-SEAL,
# the operator's act (RULE 6, LAW 6), not a sweep.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY -- corrected 2026-09-03, sitting 76.
# The first version of this file also froze `law/state/law/**` and
# `law/**/*.jsonl` on the reasoning "law/ is sealed by hash". That
# generalised from two files to a directory without checking which,
# and it was wrong twice over:
#
#   links.py:96   the chain hashes CANONICAL JSON -- sha256(prev +
#                 json.dumps(body, sort_keys=True)) -- never file bytes.
#   links.py:230  and reads the chain with universal newlines, so a CR
#                 is stripped before json.loads ever sees the line.
#
# chain.jsonl's terminator therefore cannot affect the chain. What the
# exclusion DID do was freeze a CRLF-on-disk / LF-in-HEAD mismatch into a
# file that then showed modified forever -- the exact phantom diff this
# file exists to kill, parked in the worst possible directory.
#
# Stroked by test_only_byte_hashed_law_files_are_eol_frozen.

law/*.md  -text


